WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.085
In an age of increasingly ubiquitous

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:04.879
government, surveillance,
security breaches,

00:00:04.879 --> 00:00:07.132
and high profile cancellations

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:10.552
in which internet giants have
ultimate control over users'

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.262
online profiles and data.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:15.098
Microsoft is hwlping to build a technology

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:17.892
that would give individuals
ownership rights

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:20.311
over their own virtual identities.

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.230
The mission of the ION project

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.734
is to build decentralized
identifiers or DIDs

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.360
that can serve as the anchor point

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.904
for all our activities
across the internet.

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:33.575
To achieve this level of
decentralization and immutability,

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:35.952
ION is built on top of the shared database

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:38.747
that's used for recording
Bitcoin transactions,

00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:40.665
which is known as the blockchain.

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:43.084
Though Microsoft is contributing most of the work,

00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:44.961
the company would have no control over

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:47.005
who participates in the system.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:48.590
If the goal of Bitcoin

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.509
is to eliminate the power of
central institutions over money

00:00:51.509 --> 00:00:53.595
ION is trying to do the same thing

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.889
with online identity.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:56.806
Daniel Buchner,

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:58.308
a self-described libertarian

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:00.560
is the project lead for ION.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.770
Reason sat down with him

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.564
at the Bitcoin 2021 conference in Miami

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.524
to talk about how this technology

00:01:06.524 --> 00:01:07.942
could usher in a new era

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:12.822
of hyper individualized
online identity and privacy.

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:16.034
Explain what a DID is.

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:17.202
Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:18.620
So if you're familiar,

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.997
you're all, everyone's familiar
with identity systems today.

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:23.374
I mean you use them
pretty much all day long

00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:24.793
in your digital life.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:27.712
Some of the good examples
of a digital ID today,

00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:28.880
a centralized one.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.340
are app usernames,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.050
where you log in with
username and password,

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:35.678
social auth, that's usually
backed by an email address.

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:39.432
And those forms of IDs are
essentially leased to you

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.600
by companies, right?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.062
So when you see all those
login buttons all over the web,

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:47.357
that's sort of a symptom of the disease.

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:49.234
The idea that you need to,

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:52.153
to be calling into these other authorities

00:01:52.153 --> 00:01:54.405
is to essentially prove who
you are in the digital world,

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:55.240
much like Bitcoin,

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.992
and having a Bitcoin address
backed by strong cryptography

00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:01.162
allows you to own your own monetary value.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:04.499
ION enables you to own your own IDs.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:06.126
So you can imagine an ID.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.877
It doesn't look like an email address,

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:09.295
but it's wholly yours.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.548
You can potentially log into websites.

00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:15.927
You can get credentials issued
to you that you control.

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:17.595
And you're the only one
who can prove this ID

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.264
and no company owns it.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:21.182
So there's no case

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:23.476
where someone shuts down
their email service,

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:25.687
and then you lose access to that ID

00:02:25.687 --> 00:02:27.897
or lose the ability to
log in to certain services

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:29.107
you've associated with.

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:30.817
What of, what is the issues for

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.360
like, currently now, how has,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.905
how has ION operating and
you know who who's using it?

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:36.865
How much?

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:40.034
Yeah, so we recently
announced a public preview

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:42.579
of Azure verifiable credential service.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:46.541
And the first use case of
Microsoft really is credentials.

00:02:46.541 --> 00:02:47.792
And what credentials are,

00:02:47.792 --> 00:02:49.919
is anything issued to you by anyone?

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:50.753
Right?

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:52.797
So we think a lot about like diplomas

00:02:52.797 --> 00:02:54.382
and workplace credentials, you know,

00:02:54.382 --> 00:02:55.842
proof that you're an employee.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:59.053
These things typically have been nascent.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:00.513
They don't exist in a way that you can go

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.223
and prove it to someone else.

00:03:02.223 --> 00:03:04.142
When you get your diploma,

00:03:04.142 --> 00:03:06.811
it's sort of hangs on the
wall more of like an ornament

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:08.938
than anything that you
could really go and prove.

00:03:08.938 --> 00:03:11.065
And that's bared out by looking
at LinkedIn and, you know,

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:13.610
you see all these people's
profiles, you really don't know.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:14.611
Did they go to that school?

00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:16.946
Like, is any of this information true?

00:03:16.946 --> 00:03:19.699
What verifiable credentials
and DIDs together do

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.784
it allows you to, you know, for example,

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.454
go to university and get
a digital credential,

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:25.747
signed to an ID

00:03:25.747 --> 00:03:28.583
that you can then turn around
and prove in a standard way

00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:30.627
to everyone in the digital world.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.670
And that's a, that's a huge leap, and.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.798
So, is that, that helps cut
down transaction costs, right?

00:03:35.798 --> 00:03:38.384
Because it's kind of like
an instant credit check.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.053
You are who you say you are.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:42.138
And all of the things that
you say about yourself,

00:03:42.138 --> 00:03:45.266
to the extent that people are
going to want certain things

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:46.643
to be verified, like address,

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:49.354
education, skill set, and stuff.

00:03:49.354 --> 00:03:51.814
So it just kind of eviscerates the need

00:03:51.814 --> 00:03:53.399
for figuring anything out.

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:55.193
- Yeah, it definitely is
a business optimization

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:56.945
in the sense that you, you know,

00:03:56.945 --> 00:03:58.905
what we've seen is the digitization of it,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.406
a lot of different industries.

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.408
But identity for the most part,

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.826
human identity has remained

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:07.080
paper-based, analog, you
know, long processes.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.248
And you see that all the time, right?

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.540
You're still filling out paper forms.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.125
You're still showing people these things.

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:12.835
What we're doing is saying,

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:15.964
let's, let's bring
identity to where the rest

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:18.049
of the web has already
gotten over the last 20 years

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:19.050
and really advanced things.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.388
What, what are the primary sticking points

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:25.807
to having this type of
thing more widely adopted?

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:28.977
So, really it's getting people accustomed

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:33.106
to services where they're
ultimately in control.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:34.023
And, you know,

00:04:34.023 --> 00:04:36.651
obviously as a libertarian
myself, you know,

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:37.735
I think that's a great thing.

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:40.947
I want people to be more in
control of their digital lives.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:44.284
But it also requires more
responsibility and awareness

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:45.743
because most of the services today,

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:47.996
you always have this bailout
button, which is like,

00:04:47.996 --> 00:04:49.831
you know, email recover from email

00:04:49.831 --> 00:04:52.250
or something of that nature, right?

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.376
You don't have that, right?

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:54.502
At the end of the day,

00:04:54.502 --> 00:04:56.212
an ION ID is wholly yours.

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:58.006
There is a, there is a loss condition.

